hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1216","Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr ibn al-Hakam told us, he dictated in Kufa, Musa ibn Abd al-Aziz Abu Shuaib al-Adni told us, and he is the one who is said To him: I heard Al-Qanbari, saying: I am originally Persian. He said: Al-Hakam bin Aban narrated to me, Ikrimah narrated to me, on the authority of Ibn Abbas: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said to Abbas bin Abdul Muttalib: O Abbas, O uncle, shall I not give you, shall I not reward you, shall I not do for you? Ten qualities. If you do that, God will forgive your sins, the first and the last, the old and the new, the mistake and the intentional, the small and the large. Its secret and its openness are ten characteristics: to pray four rak’ahs, reciting in each two rak’ahs the Fatihah of the Book, and a surah. If you have finished Reciting in the first rak’ah. While standing, you said: Glory be to God, and praise be to God, and there is no god but God, and God is the greatest fifteen times, then You kneel and say ten while you are bowing, then you raise your head from bowing and say ten, then you prostrate and say ten, then raise your head So you say it ten, then you prostrate and say it ten, then you raise your head and say it ten. That is seventy-five in every rak’ah that you perform in Four rak'ahs. If you are able to pray them once every day, then do so. If you cannot, then every Friday once. If you do not, then every Friday. “Once a month, and if you do not do that, then once in a year, and if you do not do that, then once in your lifetime.” It was narrated by Ibrahim bin Al-Hakam bin Aban, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Ikrimah, in a mursal form, but he did not say anything about it on the authority of Ibn Abbas. It was narrated to us by Muhammad bin Rafi’, to us by Ibrahim bin Al-Hakam."
